
Debug.Assert(Obj == null || c.Contains(Obj) && c.IndexOf(Obj) >= 0);
</code> 
The comment from the C# code is "If x is not in the array, the first and last 
positions of x are both -1."

So the Assert Statement is 
`Debug.Assert(Obj == null || c.Contains(Obj) && c.IndexOf(Obj) >= 0);`

Explanation:
In the ArrayList class, an object can be removed by either its value or by its index.
If the object (named 'Obj') is not in the list (named 'c'), then it cannot have a first position greater than or equal to '0'. Thus, the `c.IndexOf(Obj) >= 0` part of the assertion is true only if the object is not in the list.

If the object is in the list, it is also guaranteed to have a last position of '0' because it is at the index returned by `c.IndexOf(Obj)`. Also, the presence of the object in the list can be confirmed by `c.Contains(Obj)`. So overall, if any object is in the list, both `c.Contains(Obj)` and `c.IndexOf(Obj) >= 0` should be true, making the assert statement true irrespective of the object's presence or absence in the list.--------------------
